course: dictionary
lesson: 12353
title: Believable and trustworthy
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Credible means believable. Something credible can be trusted as true, or it seems likely to really happen. «The science book is credible because it shows where its facts came from.» The clue is that the book shows its proof, so you can believe it.
explain: Credible can also describe a person, a plan or a threat. «The rescue plan was credible, because the team had tested every step.» The plan seems likely to work. Ask yourself: could this really happen, and would a sensible person believe it?
explain: Do not mix up credible with incredible. Incredible now usually means amazing, as in «an incredible sunset». Credible means you can believe it.
fact: Credible means believable, able to be trusted as true.
fact: A credible person, plan or threat seems serious and likely to be real or to succeed.
fact: «A credible source» is one you can trust, such as a good encyclopedia.
fact: «A credible threat» means the danger is real and should be taken seriously.
fact: The opposite of credible is incredible, which now usually means «amazing».
exercise: «The encyclopedia is credible because it lists its sources.» What does credible mean? | *Able to be trusted as true | Far too long to read | Very funny | Written in a foreign language
exercise: «The plan was credible.» Which clue shows the plan could work? | The plan was long | The team was large | It had a colourful cover | *The team had tested every step
exercise: In everyday talk, incredible usually means the opposite of credible. What does it usually mean? | *Amazing | Believable | Trustworthy | Boring
test: «The news report was credible, because it gave the names of the reporters and the place the story came from.» What makes the report credible? | It is very short | It is read aloud at school | *It can be checked and trusted | It uses big colourful pictures
test: «The threat was credible, so the school locked its doors.» What does this show about the threat? | *It was seen as real and serious | It was about the weather | It was a fairy tale | It was a silly joke
exam: Sam says, «My cousin's story about a flying cat is not credible.» What does Sam mean? | *It could not be believed, as it is unlikely to be true | It is an amazing and wonderful story | It is far too long | It is a story about cats
